vault:os/secretBackend:SecretBackend.description:
Manages OS Secrets Engine backend configuration in a Vault server. The OS Secrets Engine manages credentials
for operating system accounts on remote hosts via SSH. This resource requires Vault 2.0.0 or later.

The OS Secrets Engine mount itself is managed separately, typically with <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`vault.Mount`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`vault.Mount`" pulumi-lang-go="`Mount`" pulumi-lang-python="`Mount`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`vault.Mount`" pulumi-lang-java="`vault.Mount`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`vault_mount`">`vault.Mount`</span>. This resource only manages
backend configuration for an existing OS mount.

Before mounting the OS Secrets Engine, the external OS plugin must already be registered in Vault's plugin catalog.
You can register it with the <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`vault.Plugin`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`vault.Plugin`" pulumi-lang-go="`Plugin`" pulumi-lang-python="`Plugin`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`vault.Plugin`" pulumi-lang-java="`vault.Plugin`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`vault_plugin`">`vault.Plugin`</span> resource.

The examples below use the canonical plugin name `vault-plugin-secrets-os`. If your Vault cluster registers the
OS plugin under a different catalog name, use that name in `vault_mount.type` instead.

See the [Vault documentation](https://www.vaultproject.io/docs/secrets/os) for more information.

